    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d475">

                <pb facs="0663" n="569" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg569"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Olney.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-01-27"><hi rendition="#italic">January 27, 1896</hi></date>.
                        (Received Feb. 7.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 586.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On Saturday last I telegraphed you the
                    substance of the reply received at the foreign office, through the colonial
                    office, to the inquiry made by me under your instructions as to the condition of
                    the American <pb facs="0664" n="570" xml:id="pg570"/> citizens held under arrest
                    by the Boer Government at the Transvaal for alleged participation in Dr.
                    Jameson’s invasion.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>The courtesy and promptness with which Her Majesty’s officials responded to our
                    inquiries respecting our countrymen is certainly marked and gratifying, and I
                    have not omitted to make acknowledgment in terms.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
